---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BreakdownTable from '../components/BreakdownTable.astro';
import { allIncidents, attributedIncidents, unverifiedIncidents, unclassifiedIncidents, snapshotMeta } from '../lib/incidents.mjs';
import { ROUTES } from '../lib/routes.mjs';
import { buildTitle, buildDescription, formatDate } from '../lib/seo.mjs';
import { MIN_INCIDENTS_FOR_PAGE } from '../lib/taxonomy/groups.mjs';

const a = snapshotMeta.attribution;
const asOf = formatDate(snapshotMeta.fetchedAt);
const rejectPct = Math.round((unverifiedIncidents.length / allIncidents.length) * 1000) / 10;

const title = buildTitle('Methodology: How Marsad Cyber Counts UAE Ransomware Attacks');
const description = buildDescription(
  `How ${allIncidents.length} raw leak-site records become ${attributedIncidents.length} attributed UAE figures: attribution rules, ${rejectPct}% rejection rate, and what this site refuses to publish.`,
);
const crumbs = [{ label: 'Home', href: ROUTES.home() }, { label: 'Methodology', href: ROUTES.methodology() }];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} crumbs={crumbs}>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Every figure on this site derives from a single dated snapshot of public leak-site
      monitoring. This page states exactly how <span class="num">{allIncidents.length}</span> raw
      records become the <span class="num">{attributedIncidents.length}</span> attributed claims
      reported elsewhere on the site, and where that process can be wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>
      The ransomware dataset is collected from <a href="https://www.ransomware.live/" rel="noopener">ransomware.live</a>,
      which monitors the public leak sites operated by ransomware groups. A record is created when
      a group publicly claims an organisation. The snapshot behind this page was taken on{' '}
      <time class="mono" datetime={snapshotMeta.fetchedAt}>{asOf}</time> and contains{' '}
      <span class="num">{snapshotMeta.recordCount}</span> records.
    </p>
    <p>
      A leak-site claim is an assertion made by an attacker. It is not a confirmed breach, it is
      not verified by the affected organisation, and it is not verified by this site. Counts on
      this site measure claims, not confirmed incidents, and that distinction is deliberate.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the headline figure is not {allIncidents.length}</h2>
    <p>
      The upstream feed tags each record with a country. That tag is not reliable. The snapshot
      behind this page includes records tagged for the United Arab Emirates whose subject
      organisation is demonstrably based elsewhere. Publishing the raw count as "attacks on the
      UAE" would overstate the figure and would not survive scrutiny.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each record is therefore classified into one of three attribution levels, using fields that
      are discarded immediately afterwards:
    </p>

    <BreakdownTable
      caption="Attribution levels applied to raw records"
      showShare={true}
      rows={[
        { slug: 'confirmed', label: 'Confirmed — organisation domain in the .ae zone', count: a.confirmed ?? 0 },
        { slug: 'probable', label: 'Probable — explicit UAE reference in the record text', count: a.probable ?? 0 },
        { slug: 'unverified', label: 'Unverified — neither signal present', count: a.unverified ?? 0 },
      ]}
    />

    <p>
      Only <strong>confirmed</strong> and <strong>probable</strong> records reach the headline
      figures, the page titles and the structured data. The{' '}
      <span class="num">{unverifiedIncidents.length}</span> unverified records, which are{' '}
      <span class="num">{rejectPct}%</span> of the feed, are excluded from every count on this
      site. They are not deleted, and they are reported here so the rejection rate is visible.
    </p>
    <p>
      This test is deliberately conservative in one direction and permissive in the other. A UAE
      organisation using a <code>.com</code> domain with no UAE reference in the record text will
      be classified unverified and dropped, so the figures on this site understate rather than
      overstate UAE ransomware activity.
    </p>

    <h2>Which date is used</h2>
    <p>
      Records carry two timestamps. This site uses <code>discovered</code>, the date the claim was
      observed on the leak site, and truncates it to the day. The date a claim was observed is not
      the date the intrusion occurred, which is typically earlier and is not published by the
      attacker. Period counts therefore describe when claims surfaced, not when compromises
      happened.
    </p>

    <h2>How sectors are assigned</h2>
    <p>
      Sector labels come from the upstream feed and are mapped to fixed slugs through an explicit
      table. An unrecognised label never creates a page: it fails the build so a person can
      classify it. Records the upstream feed left unclassified keep no sector at all. There are{' '}
      <span class="num">{unclassifiedIncidents.length}</span> such attributed records, and they
      appear in totals but in no sector breakdown, which is why sector rows sum to slightly less
      than the headline figure.
    </p>

    <h2>Which groups get their own page</h2>
    <p>
      A ransomware group earns a dedicated page once it has claimed{' '}
      <span class="num">{MIN_INCIDENTS_FOR_PAGE}</span> or more attributed UAE organisations.
      Groups seen once are listed on the{' '}
      <a href={ROUTES.groupsHub()}>groups hub</a> without a page, because a single data point
      cannot describe a pattern.
    </p>
    <p>
      Versioned operations such as LockBit 2.0 and LockBit 3.0 are counted separately. Merging
      them would be an editorial claim about actor identity rather than something recorded in the
      source data.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site does not publish</h2>
    <p>
      Affected organisations are never identified. Names, domains, leak-site URLs, leak contents,
      data volumes and employee names are removed before the data is stored, not merely omitted at
      display time, and an automated check fails the build if any of them appear in the published
      pages. The reasoning is set out in the <a href={ROUTES.editorialPolicy()}>editorial policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Known limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Counts measure attacker claims, not verified breaches.</li>
      <li>Groups that do not operate a leak site are invisible to this method entirely.</li>
      <li>Organisations that pay before publication never appear in the data.</li>
      <li>Attribution is inferred from record text, so both false positives and false negatives are possible.</li>
      <li>The current year is always partial and will rise as it completes.</li>
      <li>No emirate-level breakdown exists, because the source data carries no emirate field.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Corrections are welcome. If a figure on this site is wrong, the snapshot it came from is in
      version control and the calculation is reproducible.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
